    Embeddings
        -> Static  -> Averages token across all contextes
        -> Contextual - > Each token varies according to the context

      Word2Vec (Google, 2013) is the canonical static embedding method. It trains a shallow neural network on one of two tasks:

      -> Skip-gram: given a word, predict its surrounding context words

      - >CBOW: given surrounding context words, predict the center word

      The embeddings are a byproduct of training on this prediction task. They are the representations the network learns in order to predict context well, and they capture genuine semantic relationships as a result.

      GloVe (Global Vectors for Word Representation), developed at Stanford, achieves a similar result via a different mechanism: it factorizes the word co-occurrence matrix directly rather than training on a prediction task.

       Both approaches share a fundamental problem: every word gets exactly one vector regardless of context. "Bank" has one embedding whether it appears in "I walked along the river bank" or "I deposited money at the bank." The embedding is forced to average across both meanings, which means it captures neither perfectly.

       Sparse Embedding -> Sparse methods like B25 are fast , excel in exact keyword matching
       Dense Embedding -> Captures semantics , synonyms and paraphrases ,

       Production system use both

       The production standard is hybrid search: BM25 for first-pass candidate retrieval, dense ANN search in parallel, then Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) to merge the two ranked lists, and finally a cross-encoder re-ranker to score the top candidates jointly. This pipeline underlies the retrieval layer of most enterprise RAG systems today.






